WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.750
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:08.750
So what we want to do right now is make a new structure.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:15.750
This new structure is going to use only this little time which is positive edge of the clock

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:23.750
in which it's going to quote-unquote sample the value of the input and store it.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:29.750
The edge is going to be called positive or arising edge of the clock

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:36.990
and that is this little moment in which clock cycle goes from value of 0 to

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:43.220
value of 1. The circuit that we are going to draw right now is going to be

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:52.280
called the flip-flop and very often you will see me using DFF to annotate

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:58.520
shortly for D flip-flop. D flip-flop is going to have two inputs. One is the

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:05.640
input that is going to be data that is going to be stored and the other one is what used to be

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:12.520
enable input however we are going to elevate it right now and it's going to become clock

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:21.080
it's going to be connected to the clock that is one output that is a queue and that basically

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.720
is the value of the data that is stored.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.600
So we are going to implement this

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:42.330
using two cascading,

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:52.190
two cascading D latches.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.310
So I'm going to draw a block diagram

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.390
and point out some little things

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.070
that we need to figure out.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:07.070
So first of all, we are going to put first D latch,

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:18.100
This D latch in this configuration is going to be called master.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:28.940
This is master servant arrangement or configuration.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:45.280
So the first one is master.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:58.370
So external data input is going to be connected to the input of this latch, master latch.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:06.690
It is important to remember that we are connecting clock to the first enable, but clock has to

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:16.930
go through a node gate and I should probably put my hand down when I'm drawing things and

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.850
this is going to connect to clock.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:29.250
have Q output here and we are going to connect and I'll change the colors so

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:35.170
that you are not much that you understand that this is the second D

00:03:35.170 --> 00:04:02.220
latch this one is servant and is connected to its E output so clock is

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:08.300
connected without being negated to the E output and clock is connected with not

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:19.730
gauge to the output of the first latch and if we look into it as a box

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:35.720
this is our flip-flop. Flip-flops in general have very similar block diagram

